{"t": 1788701517.6902988, "step": 73, "turn": 227, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object."}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}, {"type": "text", "text": "Tool results:\nOccupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.91, margin 0.52)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.72)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.55)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.96, margin 0.68)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.69)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (2,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (6 of 20): (1,0), (3,0), (3,1), (3,3), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\"action\": \"spawn\", \"cell\": [1,3]}"}], "reply": "{\"action\": \"spawn\", \"cell\": [1,3]}", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": null}
{"t": 1788701626.056708, "step": 74, "turn": 228, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl1 -> eyeball\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl2 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl3 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl4 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl5 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item bloodrune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n- edge: bloodrune -> bigger pile\n  [source: popup]\n## Item bone (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1\n- description: Merge to summon a Rib Cage.\n- edge: two Bone -> Rib Cage\n- edge: bone -> rib cage\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 30\n- max_level: true\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item gems_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage/zombie when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icebox_unopened (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to open.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item lockedchest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to watch an advert to unlock this Valuable Chest.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapool_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapot_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": null, "tool_calls": [{"type": "function", "id": "direct_74", "function": {"name": "get_board_state", "arguments": "{}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "direct_74", "content": "Occupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.91, margin 0.52)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.72)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.55)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.96, margin 0.68)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.91, margin 0.49)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (2,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.92, margin 0.60)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (5 of 20): (1,0), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest merge: eyeball (2,3)+(3,3)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid merges: merge eyeball (2,3)+(3,3) | Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}], "reply": "", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": "Okay, let's break this down. The user provided the board state after a get_board_state call. First, I need to figure out what the best move is based on the rules and hints given.\n\nLooking at the occupied cells, there are several items. The key here is to check the hints from the tool response. The \"Best merge\" hint says eyeball (2,3) + (3,3). Both cells have eyeball, which is a valid merge because they're the same item. Merging two eyeballs would create an Eye Monster, which is part of the craving mentioned (eyemonster).\n\nWait, the craving is for an Eye Monster. The current state shows that there's a craving for eyemonster, so merging two eyeballs would fulfill part of that. But the hint says \"Best merge: eyeball (2,3)+(3,3)\", which makes sense because they're identical and can be merged.\n\nNow, checking if there are any other priorities. The Feats line mentions \"Beat the Peasant in combat twice\" but it's at 0/0, so not relevant. The other feat is \"Build a Supply Cupboard\" which is done. So the main focus is on merging to progress towards the craving.\n\nAlso, the board has empty cells (5 of 20), but since there's a spawnable item (supplycupboard_lvl1 at (1,3)), maybe spawning could be an option. However, the Best merge hint is prioritized here. The user's instructions say to follow the hints unless there's a better move.\n\nAnother thing: the mana bar is ~100% full, so collecting isn't recommended. Feeding is possible but the satiety is 648/2000 (1352 remaining), which is a lot. The best feed would be for the craving, but since the craving is for an Eye Monster, merging the eyeballs is the way to go.\n\nWait, the current state shows that there are two eyeballs at (2,3) and (3,3). Merging them would create an Eye Monster, which is the craved item. So this merge directly contributes to fulfilling the craving. The hint says \"Best merge\" so it's the top priority.\n\nAlso, checking if there's a conflict with other rules. The cells are both [FEEDABLE], but merging them is allowed since they're the same item. No max-level items here that can't be merged"}
{"t": 1788701660.461171, "step": 74, "turn": 229, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl1 -> eyeball\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl2 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl3 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl4 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl5 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item bloodrune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n- edge: bloodrune -> bigger pile\n  [source: popup]\n## Item bone (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1\n- description: Merge to summon a Rib Cage.\n- edge: two Bone -> Rib Cage\n- edge: bone -> rib cage\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 30\n- max_level: true\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item gems_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage/zombie when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icebox_unopened (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to open.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item lockedchest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to watch an advert to unlock this Valuable Chest.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapool_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapot_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": null, "tool_calls": [{"type": "function", "id": "direct_74", "function": {"name": "get_board_state", "arguments": "{}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "direct_74", "content": "Occupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.91, margin 0.52)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.72)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.55)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.96, margin 0.68)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.91, margin 0.49)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (2,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.92, margin 0.60)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (5 of 20): (1,0), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest merge: eyeball (2,3)+(3,3)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid merges: merge eyeball (2,3)+(3,3) | Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}, {"role": "user", "content": "If you need several of these, call them ALL in this round (multiple tool calls at once) instead of one per round."}], "reply": "", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": "Okay, let's break this down. The user provided the board state after a get_board_state call. I need to figure out the best move based on the rules and the current situation.\n\nFirst, looking at the occupied cells. There are several items here. The key points from the board state:\n\n- Empty cells: (1,0), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1) \u2014 5 empty spots.\n- Best merge hint is eyeball (2,3) + (3,3). Both are labeled as [FEEDABLE] with high scores. The merge would create an Eye Monster, which is the current craving (since the craving bubble says \"eyemonster\"). \n\nWait, the craving is for an Eye Monster. So merging two eyeballs would give a level 1 Eye Monster? But the user's board state shows that the craving is \"eyemonster\" with progress 0/0. Wait, maybe the current craving is for the Eye Monster, so feeding it would count toward the quota. But the Best merge hint is pointing to merging two eyeballs into an Eye Monster.\n\nBut according to the rules, when there's a craving, you should feed the exact level of the craved item. However, if the best merge is available and it's towards fulfilling the craving, maybe merging is better. Wait, but the user's board state says \"Cravings (bubble): eyemonster\". So the current craving is for an Eye Monster. The Eye Monster is a creature, so to fulfill the craving, you need to feed an Eye Monster. But the board has two eyeballs (which are level 1), and merging them would create an Eye Monster (level 1). Wait, but the merge chain: according to the item glossary, two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nSo if you merge the two eyeballs at (2,3) and (3,3), you get an Eye Monster. But the craving is for an Eye Monster, so feeding that would count toward the quota. However, the Best merge hint is suggesting merging them. But the problem is, the user's current state shows that the Feats line has \"Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster\" as a feat, but it's not completed yet.\n\nWait, the board state says: \"Feats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Fe"}
{"t": 1788701670.4355042, "step": 74, "turn": 230, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object."}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}, {"type": "text", "text": "Tool results:\nOccupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.91, margin 0.52)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.72)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.55)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.96, margin 0.68)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.91, margin 0.49)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (2,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.92, margin 0.60)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (5 of 20): (1,0), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest merge: eyeball (2,3)+(3,3)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid merges: merge eyeball (2,3)+(3,3) | Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [2, 3], \"b\": [3, 3]}"}], "reply": "{\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [2, 3], \"b\": [3, 3]}", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": null}
{"t": 1788701782.4266372, "step": 75, "turn": 231, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl1 -> eyeball\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl2 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl3 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl4 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl5 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item bloodrune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n- edge: bloodrune -> bigger pile\n  [source: popup]\n## Item bone (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1\n- description: Merge to summon a Rib Cage.\n- edge: two Bone -> Rib Cage\n- edge: bone -> rib cage\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 30\n- max_level: true\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item gems_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage/zombie when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icebox_unopened (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to open.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item lockedchest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to watch an advert to unlock this Valuable Chest.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapool_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapot_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": null, "tool_calls": [{"type": "function", "id": "direct_75", "function": {"name": "get_board_state", "arguments": "{}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "direct_75", "content": "Occupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.92, margin 0.53)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.68)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.56)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 1.00, margin 0.72)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.69)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.92, margin 0.50)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (6 of 20): (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}], "reply": "", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out the best move here. First, I need to look at the board state provided.\n\nThe user has given me the current board state. Let me parse through it carefully.\n\nOccupied cells:\n- (0,0): coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE]\n- (0,1): coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (0,2): manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) [FEEDABLE]\n- (0,3): necromerger [UNKNOWN] \u2014 don't feed\n- (1,1): gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (1,2): cauldron [UNKNOWN] \u2014 don't feed\n- (1,3): supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE]\n- (2,0): bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE]\n- (2,1): bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE]\n- (2,2): ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (3,2): icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN] \u2014 but it's spawnable\n- (3,3): eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (4,2): grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE]\n- (4,3): grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE]\n\nEmpty cells: 6 of 20. The best spawn hint is icebox_unopened at (3,2), which can spawn icerune/poisonrune. But wait, the user mentioned \"Best spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\".\n\nWait, but there's also a supplycupboard_lvl1 at (1,3). However, the best spawn is listed as icebox_unopened. Let me check the spawnable cells again.\n\nThe board has two spawn stations: supplycupboard_lvl1 (1,3) and grave_lvl1 (4,2), grave_lvl3 (4,3), and icebox_unopened (3,2). But the best spawn hint is for icebox_unopened. However, the user's tool result says \"Best spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\".\n\nBut wait, the"}
{"t": 1788701817.343642, "step": 75, "turn": 232, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl1 -> eyeball\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl2 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl3 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl4 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl5 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item bloodrune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n- edge: bloodrune -> bigger pile\n  [source: popup]\n## Item bone (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1\n- description: Merge to summon a Rib Cage.\n- edge: two Bone -> Rib Cage\n- edge: bone -> rib cage\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 30\n- max_level: true\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item gems_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage/zombie when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icebox_unopened (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to open.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item lockedchest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to watch an advert to unlock this Valuable Chest.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapool_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapot_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": null, "tool_calls": [{"type": "function", "id": "direct_75", "function": {"name": "get_board_state", "arguments": "{}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "direct_75", "content": "Occupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.92, margin 0.53)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.68)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.56)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 1.00, margin 0.72)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.69)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.92, margin 0.50)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (6 of 20): (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}, {"role": "user", "content": "If you need several of these, call them ALL in this round (multiple tool calls at once) instead of one per round."}], "reply": "", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out the best move here. First, I need to look at the board state provided.\n\nThe user has given me the current board state with all the occupied cells and their labels. Let me go through each part step by step.\n\nFirst, the occupied cells:\n\n- (0,0): coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE]\n- (0,1): coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (0,2): manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) [FEEDABLE]\n- (0,3): necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (can't feed)\n- (1,1): gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (1,2): cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed]\n- (1,3): supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE]\n- (2,0): bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE]\n- (2,1): bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE]\n- (2,2): ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (3,2): icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE]\n- (3,3): eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (4,2): grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE]\n- (4,3): grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE]\n\nEmpty cells: (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1) \u2014 6 empty cells.\n\nThe best spawn hint is icebox_unopened at (3,2) which can spawn icerune/poisonrune. But wait, the user mentioned \"Best spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\". So there are 6 empty cells, which is a lot.\n\nBut looking at the Feats line: \"Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\" So that feat is already completed. The other feats are about beating the Peasant twice and merging things 250 times, but they're not done yet.\n\nThe current craving is eyemonster. The user has an eyeinajar at (3,3), which"}
{"t": 1788701827.121121, "step": 75, "turn": 233, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object."}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}, {"type": "text", "text": "Tool results:\nOccupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.92, margin 0.53)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.68)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.56)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 1.00, margin 0.72)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.69)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.92, margin 0.50)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (6 of 20): (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\"action\": \"spawn\", \"cell\": [1,3]}"}], "reply": "{\"action\": \"spawn\", \"cell\": [1,3]}", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": null}
{"t": 1788701977.401991, "step": 76, "turn": 234, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl1 -> eyeball\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl2 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl3 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl4 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl5 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item bloodrune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n- edge: bloodrune -> bigger pile\n  [source: popup]\n## Item bone (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1\n- description: Merge to summon a Rib Cage.\n- edge: two Bone -> Rib Cage\n- edge: bone -> rib cage\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 30\n- max_level: true\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item gems_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage/zombie when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icebox_unopened (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to open.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item lockedchest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to watch an advert to unlock this Valuable Chest.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapool_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapot_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": null, "tool_calls": [{"type": "function", "id": "direct_76", "function": {"name": "get_board_state", "arguments": "{}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "direct_76", "content": "Occupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.90, margin 0.51)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.72)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.55)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.96, margin 0.68)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.91, margin 0.49)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (2,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.70, margin 0.41)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (5 of 20): (1,0), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (5 empty)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}], "reply": "", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out the best move here. First, I need to look at the board state provided.\n\nThe user has given me the current board state. Let me parse through all the details.\n\nOccupied cells: There are several items listed with their IDs, feed values, and whether they're feedable or spawnable. Empty cells are (1,0), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1) \u2014 so 5 empty spots. The bottom bar shows that the Feats line has \"Beat the Peasant in combat twice\" which is a champion combat feat. Also, there's a craving for an Eye Monster.\n\nLooking at the hints: Best spawn is icebox_unopened at (3,2), which can spawn icerune/poisonrune. But wait, the user mentioned that the best spawn hint is for icebox_unopened. However, the Feats line says \"Beat the Peasant in combat twice\" which is a champion combat feat. The board has a champion (Peasant) since the Feats mention it.\n\nWait, the Feats line says: [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed). So there's a champion on the board (the Peasant), and the goal is to beat them twice. But the current state shows that the Peasant hasn't been defeated yet (since it's 0/2).\n\nBut looking at the occupied cells, there's an eyeball (feed 15) and other items. The user also has a \"Best attack\" hint if a champion is present. Since the Feats mention beating the Peasant twice, and the board state shows that the Peasant is present (since it's a champion), then attacking would be a priority.\n\nWait, but the current board state doesn't explicitly say there's a Peasant on the board. Wait, the Feats line says \"Beat the Peasant in combat twice\", which implies that the Peasant is the current champion. So the Peasant must be on the board. But how do we know? The user's tool result mentions \"Champion: The Peasant\" but maybe it's not shown here.\n\nWait, the Feats line says \"Beat the Peasant in combat twice\", which is a feat that requires defeating the Peasant. So the Peasant must be on the board as a champion. Therefore, there's a champion present (the Peasant), and"}
{"t": 1788702012.430934, "step": 76, "turn": 235, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl1 -> eyeball\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl2 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl3 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl4 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl5 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item bloodrune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n- edge: bloodrune -> bigger pile\n  [source: popup]\n## Item bone (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1\n- description: Merge to summon a Rib Cage.\n- edge: two Bone -> Rib Cage\n- edge: bone -> rib cage\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 30\n- max_level: true\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item gems_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage/zombie when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icebox_unopened (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to open.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item lockedchest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to watch an advert to unlock this Valuable Chest.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapool_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapot_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": null, "tool_calls": [{"type": "function", "id": "direct_76", "function": {"name": "get_board_state", "arguments": "{}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "direct_76", "content": "Occupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.90, margin 0.51)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.72)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.55)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.96, margin 0.68)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.91, margin 0.49)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (2,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.70, margin 0.41)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (5 of 20): (1,0), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (5 empty)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}, {"role": "user", "content": "If you need several of these, call them ALL in this round (multiple tool calls at once) instead of one per round."}], "reply": "", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out the best move here. First, I need to look at the board state provided.\n\nThe user has given me the current board state with all the occupied cells and their labels. Let me go through each part step by step.\n\nFirst, the empty cells are (1,0), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1). There are 5 empty cells out of 20 total. The board is 5x4, so 20 cells. That's a bit sparse, but not too bad.\n\nNow, looking at the occupied cells:\n\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2: feed 5, FEEDABLE\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1: feed 2, FEEDABLE\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1: feed 60, dmg 25\n- (0,3) necromerger: UNKNOWN, don't feed\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1: feed 2, FEEDABLE\n- (1,2) cauldron: UNKNOWN, don't feed\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1: SPAWNABLE\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2: feed 5, FEEDABLE\n- (2,1) bone: feed 1, FEEDABLE\n- (2,2) ribcage: feed 2, FEEDABLE\n- (2,3) eyeball: feed 15, FEEDABLE\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened: UNKNOWN, but SPAWNABLE\n- (3,3) eyeinajar: feed 2, FEEDABLE\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1: SPAWNABLE\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3: SPAWNABLE\n\nThe bottom bar shows that the user has a \"Best spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (5 empty)\". Also, there's a craving for an Eye Monster. The Feats line mentions \"Build a Supply Cupboard\" is done, and \"Beat the Peasant in combat twice\" is 0/0.\n\nWait, the user has a champion present because the suggested direction is champion_combat. But looking at the board state, there's no mention of a champion cell. Wait, the Feats line says \"Suggested direction"}
{"t": 1788702022.435853, "step": 76, "turn": 236, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object."}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}, {"type": "text", "text": "Tool results:\nOccupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.90, margin 0.51)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.72)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.55)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.96, margin 0.68)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.91, margin 0.49)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (2,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.70, margin 0.41)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (5 of 20): (1,0), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (5 empty)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\"action\": \"spawn\", \"cell\": [1, 3]}"}], "reply": "{\"action\": \"spawn\", \"cell\": [1, 3]}", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": null}
{"t": 1788702132.8251488, "step": 77, "turn": 237, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl1 -> eyeball\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl2 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl3 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl4 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl5 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item bloodrune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n- edge: bloodrune -> bigger pile\n  [source: popup]\n## Item bone (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1\n- description: Merge to summon a Rib Cage.\n- edge: two Bone -> Rib Cage\n- edge: bone -> rib cage\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 30\n- max_level: true\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item gems_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage/zombie when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icebox_unopened (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to open.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item lockedchest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to watch an advert to unlock this Valuable Chest.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapool_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapot_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": null, "tool_calls": [{"type": "function", "id": "direct_77", "function": {"name": "get_board_state", "arguments": "{}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "direct_77", "content": "Occupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.91, margin 0.53)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.71)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.56)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.96, margin 0.68)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.68)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (2,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.57)\n- (3,1) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.92, margin 0.55)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.70, margin 0.41)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (4 of 20): (1,0), (3,0), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest merge: eyeball (2,3)+(3,1)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid merges: merge eyeball (2,3)+(3,1) | Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}], "reply": "", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": "Okay, let's break this down. The user provided the board state, and I need to figure out the best move.\n\nFirst, looking at the occupied cells. There are several items here. The key points from the board state:\n\n- Empty cells: (1,0), (3,0), (4,0), (4,1) \u2014 so 4 empty spots.\n- Best merge hint is eyeball (2,3) and (3,1). Both are feedable, but merging them would create an Eye Monster. Wait, the craving is for an Eye Monster (eyemonster), so maybe that's a good move.\n\nWait, the Feats line says \"Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster\" but it's 0/0. The current state has eyeballs and eyeinajar. The merge of two eyeballs would make an Eye Monster. But the craving is for eyemonster, which might be the next level. Let me check the item list.\n\nFrom the items learned: \"eyeinajar (feed 2)\" and \"eyeball (feed 15)\". The merge chain for eye monster probably involves combining eyeballs. Wait, the popup for eyeball says \"Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\" So two eyeballs would make an Eye Monster. But the current board has two eyeballs: one at (2,3) and (3,1). Wait, no: looking at the occupied cells:\n\n- (2,3) is eyeball (feed 15)\n- (3,1) is eyeball (feed 15)\n\nSo merging those two would create an Eye Monster. The craving is for eyemonster, so this merge would fulfill part of the craving. But the Feats line says \"Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster\" which might be a different level.\n\nWait, the board state shows \"Cravings (bubble): eyemonster\". So the current craving is to get an Eye Monster. If merging two eyeballs gives an Eye Monster, then that would satisfy the craving. But the Feats line says \"Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster\" which might be a different target.\n\nBut according to the board state, the Best merge hint is exactly those two eyeballs. So merging them would create an Eye Monster, which is the current craving. However, the user's instructions say that feeding the craved item (at exact level) is preferred. But here, the merge would create the craved item.\n\nWait, but"}
{"t": 1788702167.454863, "step": 77, "turn": 238, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl1 -> eyeball\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl2 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl3 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl4 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl5 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item bloodrune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n- edge: bloodrune -> bigger pile\n  [source: popup]\n## Item bone (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1\n- description: Merge to summon a Rib Cage.\n- edge: two Bone -> Rib Cage\n- edge: bone -> rib cage\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 30\n- max_level: true\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item gems_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage/zombie when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icebox_unopened (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to open.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item lockedchest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to watch an advert to unlock this Valuable Chest.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapool_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapot_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": null, "tool_calls": [{"type": "function", "id": "direct_77", "function": {"name": "get_board_state", "arguments": "{}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "direct_77", "content": "Occupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.91, margin 0.53)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.71)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.56)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.96, margin 0.68)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.68)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (2,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.57)\n- (3,1) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.92, margin 0.55)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.70, margin 0.41)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (4 of 20): (1,0), (3,0), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest merge: eyeball (2,3)+(3,1)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid merges: merge eyeball (2,3)+(3,1) | Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}, {"role": "user", "content": "If you need several of these, call them ALL in this round (multiple tool calls at once) instead of one per round."}], "reply": "", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": "Okay, let's break this down. The user provided the board state after a get_board_state call. I need to figure out the best move based on the rules and the current situation.\n\nFirst, looking at the occupied cells. There are several items here. The key points from the board state:\n\n- Empty cells: (1,0), (3,0), (4,0), (4,1) \u2014 four empty spots.\n- Best merge hint is eyeball (2,3) and (3,1). Both are labeled as [FEEDABLE], so they can be merged. Wait, but the merge chain for eyeball: from the item list, eyeball (feed 15) can merge into an Eye Monster. The best merge hint says to merge those two eyeballs. But wait, the user's board state shows two eyeballs at (2,3) and (3,1). Each has a feed value of 15. Merging them would create an Eye Monster, which is probably the next step for the craving.\n\nWait, the current craving is \"eyemonster\" as per the board state. So the user needs to fulfill that craving. The craving is for an Eye Monster, and the best merge hint is merging two eyeballs. But the problem is, the current state shows two eyeballs (each feed 15), so merging them would create an Eye Monster. However, the Feats line mentions \"Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster\" as a feat, but it's not completed yet.\n\nWait, the board state says: \"Feats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\"\n\nSo the \"Build a Supply Cupboard\" is done, which makes sense because supplycupboard_lvl1 is on the board. The current craving is for an Eye Monster, so merging two eyeballs would create one. But the user needs to check if that's the best move.\n\nWait, but the Feats line also has \"Beat the Peasant in combat twice\" as a feat. However, the board state shows no champion present? Wait, the bottom bar says \"Suggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions"}
{"t": 1788702177.5197, "step": 77, "turn": 239, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object."}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}, {"type": "text", "text": "Tool results:\nOccupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.91, margin 0.53)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.71)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.56)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.96, margin 0.68)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.68)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (2,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.57)\n- (3,1) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.92, margin 0.55)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.70, margin 0.41)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (4 of 20): (1,0), (3,0), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest merge: eyeball (2,3)+(3,1)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid merges: merge eyeball (2,3)+(3,1) | Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [2, 3], \"b\": [3, 1]}"}], "reply": "{\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [2, 3], \"b\": [3, 1]}", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": null}
{"t": 1788702287.458613, "step": 78, "turn": 240, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl1 -> eyeball\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl2 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl3 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl4 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl5 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item bloodrune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n- edge: bloodrune -> bigger pile\n  [source: popup]\n## Item bone (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1\n- description: Merge to summon a Rib Cage.\n- edge: two Bone -> Rib Cage\n- edge: bone -> rib cage\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 30\n- max_level: true\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item gems_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage/zombie when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icebox_unopened (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to open.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item lockedchest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to watch an advert to unlock this Valuable Chest.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapool_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapot_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": null, "tool_calls": [{"type": "function", "id": "direct_78", "function": {"name": "get_board_state", "arguments": "{}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "direct_78", "content": "Occupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.92, margin 0.53)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.68)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.56)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 1.00, margin 0.72)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.69)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (3,1) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.39)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.70, margin 0.41)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.83, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (5 of 20): (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest merge: eyeinajar (3,1)+(3,3)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid merges: merge eyeinajar (3,1)+(3,3) | Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}], "reply": "", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": "Okay, let's break this down. The user provided the board state, and I need to figure out the best move.\n\nFirst, looking at the occupied cells. There are several items here. The key points from the board state:\n\n- There's a craving for an Eye Monster (eyemonster). The current state shows that the Devourer has a craving for an Eye Monster, which is probably the highest level possible here. \n\nThe \"Best merge\" hint says to merge eyeinajar at (3,1) and (3,3). Both are labeled as [FEEDABLE] with feed values of 2 each. Merging two eyeinajar would create a higher-level Eye Monster, which is exactly what the craving needs. Since the craving is for an Eye Monster, merging these two should fulfill part of the requirement.\n\nWait, but the Feats line mentions \"Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster\" as a feat. But the current state shows that the user hasn't built it yet. However, the board has eyeinajar (which are lower level) and the craving is for the Eye Monster. So merging two eyeinajar would create an Eye Monster, which would count toward the craving.\n\nBut wait, the merge chain: according to the item glossary, eyeinajar is part of the supplycupboard chain. The description says \"Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\" So two eyeinajar should merge into an Eye Monster. That's correct.\n\nNow, checking if there are any other priorities. The Feats line has \"Beat the Peasant in combat twice\" which is 0/0 completed. But since the user hasn't done that yet, maybe it's not urgent. However, the current state shows a champion (Peasant) is present, but the board state doesn't explicitly mention a champion cell. Wait, the Feats line says \"Beat the Peasant in combat twice\" which implies there's a Peasant on the board. But looking at the occupied cells, there's no mention of a Peasant. Hmm.\n\nWait, the user mentioned \"Suggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\" So there is a champion present. But in the occupied cells list, there's no cell labeled as a champion. Wait, maybe the champion is on the board but not listed here? Or perhaps the Peasant is a champion that's not visible as an item. The user says \"The Peasant is a small human sprite \u2014"}
{"t": 1788702322.4281678, "step": 78, "turn": 241, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl1 -> eyeball\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl2 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl3 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl4 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl5 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item bloodrune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n- edge: bloodrune -> bigger pile\n  [source: popup]\n## Item bone (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1\n- description: Merge to summon a Rib Cage.\n- edge: two Bone -> Rib Cage\n- edge: bone -> rib cage\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 30\n- max_level: true\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item gems_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage/zombie when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icebox_unopened (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to open.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item lockedchest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to watch an advert to unlock this Valuable Chest.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapool_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapot_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": null, "tool_calls": [{"type": "function", "id": "direct_78", "function": {"name": "get_board_state", "arguments": "{}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "direct_78", "content": "Occupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.92, margin 0.53)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.68)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.56)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 1.00, margin 0.72)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.69)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (3,1) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.39)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.70, margin 0.41)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.83, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (5 of 20): (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest merge: eyeinajar (3,1)+(3,3)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid merges: merge eyeinajar (3,1)+(3,3) | Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}, {"role": "user", "content": "If you need several of these, call them ALL in this round (multiple tool calls at once) instead of one per round."}], "reply": "", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": "Okay, let's see. The user provided the board state and wants me to decide the best move. Let me go through the details.\n\nFirst, looking at the occupied cells. There are several items here. The key points from the board state:\n\n- Empty cells: (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (4,0), (4,1). So there are 5 empty spots.\n- Best merge hint is eyeinajar (3,1) and (3,3). Both are labeled as [FEEDABLE] with feed values of 2 each. Merging them would create an Eye Monster, which is the current craving. The craving is for an Eye Monster, so merging these two should fulfill part of the craving.\n\nWait, the craving is \"eyemonster\" and the board shows a Best merge hint for eyeinajar (3,1) and (3,3). Since the user's strategy is to prioritize champion_combat, but the current craving is active. The Feats line mentions \"Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster\" which is probably the goal here.\n\nBut the main thing is that merging two eyeinajar would create an Eye Monster. However, the Eye Monster is the craved item. Wait, the merge chain for eyeinajar: according to the item list, supplycupboard_lvl1 spawns eyeball, and then maybe higher levels. But the user has eyeinajar (3,1) and (3,3). The description says \"eyeinajar\" is a feedable item with feed value 2. Merging two of them would create an Eye Monster (since the chain is eyeball -> eyeinajar -> Eye Monster).\n\nWait, looking at the item list: \"Item eyeball (popup)\" has merge chain to Eye Monster. So merging two eyeinajar might not be the right step. Wait, maybe the Eye Monster is the next level. Let me check.\n\nThe user's board state shows that the Best merge is eyeinajar (3,1) + (3,3). The current craving is for an Eye Monster. So if you merge two eyeinajar, do they form an Eye Monster? Let me check the item list again.\n\nIn the items learned: \"Item eyeball (popup)\" has description \"Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\" Then \"Item eyeinajar (popup)\" says \"Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\" So maybe eyeinajar is the level 1 of"}
{"t": 1788702332.383637, "step": 78, "turn": 242, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object."}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}, {"type": "text", "text": "Tool results:\nOccupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.92, margin 0.53)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.68)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.56)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 1.00, margin 0.72)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.69)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (3,1) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.39)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeinajar (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.70, margin 0.41)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.83, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (5 of 20): (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest merge: eyeinajar (3,1)+(3,3)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid merges: merge eyeinajar (3,1)+(3,3) | Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [3, 1], \"b\": [3, 3]}"}], "reply": "{\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [3, 1], \"b\": [3, 3]}", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": null}
{"t": 1788702443.8676622, "step": 79, "turn": 243, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl1 -> eyeball\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl2 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl3 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl4 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl5 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item bloodrune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n- edge: bloodrune -> bigger pile\n  [source: popup]\n## Item bone (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1\n- description: Merge to summon a Rib Cage.\n- edge: two Bone -> Rib Cage\n- edge: bone -> rib cage\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 30\n- max_level: true\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item gems_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage/zombie when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icebox_unopened (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to open.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item lockedchest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to watch an advert to unlock this Valuable Chest.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapool_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapot_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": null, "tool_calls": [{"type": "function", "id": "direct_79", "function": {"name": "get_board_state", "arguments": "{}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "direct_79", "content": "Occupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.92, margin 0.53)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.68)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.56)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 1.00, margin 0.72)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.69)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.58)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (6 of 20): (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}], "reply": "", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out the best move here. First, I need to look at the board state provided.\n\nThe user has given me the current board state. Let me parse through it carefully.\n\nOccupied cells:\n- (0,0): coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE]\n- (0,1): coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (0,2): manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) [FEEDABLE]\n- (0,3): necromerger [UNKNOWN] \u2014 don't feed\n- (1,1): gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (1,2): cauldron [UNKNOWN] \u2014 don't feed\n- (1,3): supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE]\n- (2,0): bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE]\n- (2,1): bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE]\n- (2,2): ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (3,2): icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN] \u2014 but it's spawnable\n- (3,3): eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE]\n- (4,2): grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE]\n- (4,3): grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE]\n\nEmpty cells: 6 of 20. The best spawn hint is icebox_unopened at (3,2), which can spawn icerune/poisonrune. But wait, the user mentioned \"Best spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\".\n\nWait, but there's also a supplycupboard_lvl1 at (1,3). However, the best spawn is listed as icebox_unopened. Let me check the spawnable cells again.\n\nThe board has two spawn stations: supplycupboard_lvl1 (1,3) and grave_lvl1 (4,2), grave_lvl3 (4,3), and icebox_unopened (3,2). But the best spawn hint is for icebox_unopened. However, the user's tool result says \"Best spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\".\n\nBut wait, the"}
{"t": 1788702478.9328299, "step": 79, "turn": 244, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl1 -> eyeball\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl2 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl3 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl4 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl5 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item bloodrune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n- edge: bloodrune -> bigger pile\n  [source: popup]\n## Item bone (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1\n- description: Merge to summon a Rib Cage.\n- edge: two Bone -> Rib Cage\n- edge: bone -> rib cage\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 30\n- max_level: true\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item gems_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage/zombie when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icebox_unopened (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to open.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item lockedchest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to watch an advert to unlock this Valuable Chest.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapool_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapot_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": null, "tool_calls": [{"type": "function", "id": "direct_79", "function": {"name": "get_board_state", "arguments": "{}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "direct_79", "content": "Occupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.92, margin 0.53)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.68)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.56)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 1.00, margin 0.72)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.69)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.58)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (6 of 20): (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}, {"role": "user", "content": "If you need several of these, call them ALL in this round (multiple tool calls at once) instead of one per round."}], "reply": "", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out the best move here. First, I need to look at the board state provided.\n\nThe user has given me the current board state with all the occupied cells and their labels. Let me go through each part step by step.\n\nFirst, the occupied cells:\n\n- (0,0): coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE]\n- (0,1): coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (0,2): manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) [FEEDABLE]\n- (0,3): necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (can't feed)\n- (1,1): gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (1,2): cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed]\n- (1,3): supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE]\n- (2,0): bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE]\n- (2,1): bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE]\n- (2,2): ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE]\n- (3,2): icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE]\n- (3,3): eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE]\n- (4,2): grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE]\n- (4,3): grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE]\n\nEmpty cells: (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1) \u2014 6 empty cells.\n\nThe best spawn hint is icebox_unopened at (3,2) which can spawn icerune/poisonrune. But wait, the user mentioned \"Best spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\". So there are 6 empty cells, which is good for spawning.\n\nBut also, there's a supplycupboard_lvl1 at (1,3), which is another spawnable cell. However, the best spawn hint points to icebox_unopened. But why?\n\nWait, the user says \"Best spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\". So"}
{"t": 1788702488.843798, "step": 79, "turn": 245, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object."}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}, {"type": "text", "text": "Tool results:\nOccupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.92, margin 0.53)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.68)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.56)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 1.00, margin 0.72)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.69)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.58)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (6 of 20): (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest spawn: icebox_unopened (3,2) -> icerune/poisonrune (6 empty)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "{\"action\": \"spawn\", \"cell\": [1,3]}"}], "reply": "{\"action\": \"spawn\", \"cell\": [1,3]}", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": null}
{"t": 1788702600.54233, "step": 80, "turn": 246, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl1 -> eyeball\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl2 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl3 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl4 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item supplycupboard_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: supplycupboard_lvl5 -> eyeball, eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item bloodrune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n- edge: bloodrune -> bigger pile\n  [source: popup]\n## Item bone (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1\n- description: Merge to summon a Rib Cage.\n- edge: two Bone -> Rib Cage\n- edge: bone -> rib cage\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item coins_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 30\n- max_level: true\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item gems_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- description: Spawns bone/ribcage/zombie when tapped.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icebox_unopened (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to open.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerune_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 12\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item lockedchest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to watch an advert to unlock this Valuable Chest.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapool_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapot_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- description: Increases Mana Cap. Merge to lvl up.\n- edge: Mana Cap +2000\n  [source: popup]\n## Item manapotion_lvl3 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 22\n- max_level: true\n- description: Feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]"}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "[image]"}}, {"type": "text", "text": "This is the current board. The board state is already provided below (see Tool results) \u2014 decide the single best move and reply with ONLY a valid JSON action (merge, spawn, feed, attack, collect, or idle). There are no tools in this round \u2014 never emit a tool call or a tool name as the action."}]}, {"role": "assistant", "content": null, "tool_calls": [{"type": "function", "id": "direct_80", "function": {"name": "get_board_state", "arguments": "{}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "direct_80", "content": "Occupied cells (template bank labels):\n- (0,0) coins_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.96, margin 0.63)\n- (0,1) coins_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.60)\n- (0,2) manapool_lvl1 (feed 60) (dmg 25) (score 0.91, margin 0.54)\n- (0,3) necromerger [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.97, margin 0.71)\n- (1,1) gems_lvl1 (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.97, margin 0.56)\n- (1,2) cauldron [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] (score 0.96, margin 0.62)\n- (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.91, margin 0.54)\n- (2,0) bloodrune_lvl2 (feed 5) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.54)\n- (2,1) bone (feed 1) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.99, margin 0.57)\n- (2,2) ribcage (feed 2) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.57)\n- (2,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 1.00, margin 0.57)\n- (3,2) icebox_unopened [UNKNOWN VALUE \u2014 do not feed] [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.93, margin 0.67)\n- (3,3) eyeball (feed 15) [FEEDABLE] (score 0.94, margin 0.53)\n- (4,2) grave_lvl1 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.84, margin 0.48)\n- (4,3) grave_lvl3 [SPAWNABLE] (score 0.98, margin 0.69)\nUnidentified occupied cells: []\nEmpty cells (5 of 20): (1,0), (3,0), (3,1), (4,0), (4,1)\nBest merge: eyeball (2,3)+(3,3)\nMana: ~100% full\nSatiety: 648/2000 (1352 remaining)\nCravings (bubble): eyemonster\nBottom bar: unlocked feats,station,queue(reward),shop  locked spellbook\nFeats (tier 5): [neutral] Beat the Peasant in combat twice. (0/0 Feats Completed); [merge] Merge things 250 times. (0/0) [other]; [merge] Own a lvl 5 Eye Monster. (0/0) [other]; Build a Supply Cupboard. (done).\nSuggested direction: champion_combat (champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack).\nChoose ONLY from: Valid merges: merge eyeball (2,3)+(3,3) | Valid spawns: spawn (1,3) supplycupboard_lvl1; spawn (3,2) icebox_unopened \u2014 do NOT repeat any rejected move.\nDecision checklist: 1. Pick ONLY a move from the Choose ONLY from line (or a Best hint); prefer [FEEDABLE]/[SPAWNABLE] cells. 2. Never repeat a rejected move; never merge [NEVER MERGE] cells; never output a tool name. 3. Reply with ONLY the JSON action."}], "reply": "", "tool_calls": null, "reasoning": "Okay, let's break this down. The user provided the board state after a get_board_state call. First, I need to figure out what the best move is based on the rules and hints given.\n\nLooking at the occupied cells, there are several items. The key here is to check the hints from the tool response. The \"Best merge\" hint says eyeball (2,3) + (3,3). Both cells have eyeball, which is a valid merge because they're the same item. Merging two eyeballs would create an Eye Monster, which is part of the craving mentioned (eyemonster).\n\nWait, the craving is for an Eye Monster. The current state shows that there's a craving for eyemonster, so merging two eyeballs would fulfill part of that. But the hint says \"Best merge: eyeball (2,3)+(3,3)\", which makes sense because they're identical and can be merged.\n\nNow, checking if there are any other priorities. The Feats line mentions \"Beat the Peasant in combat twice\" but it's at 0/0, so not relevant. The other feat is \"Build a Supply Cupboard\" which is done. So the main focus is on merging to progress towards the craving.\n\nAlso, the board has empty cells (5 of 20), but since there's a spawn station (supplycupboard_lvl1 at (1,3)), maybe spawning could be an option. However, the Best merge hint is prioritized here. The user's instructions say to follow the hints unless there's a better move.\n\nAnother thing: the mana bar is ~100% full, so collecting isn't recommended. Feeding is possible but the satiety is 648/2000 (1352 remaining), which is a lot. The best feed would be for the craving, but since the craving is for an Eye Monster, merging the eyeballs to get it is better.\n\nWait, the current state has two eyeballs (at (2,3) and (3,3)), so merging them would create an Eye Monster. But the problem is whether the Eye Monster is the craved item. The craving is \"eyemonster\", which is the next level. So merging two eyeballs should give that.\n\nBut the user's instructions say to avoid feeding precursors. However, in this case, the precursor for the Eye Monster is the eyeball (since it's a lower level). Wait, the merge"}
{"t": 1788702635.165146, "step": 80, "turn": 247, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the brain of a bot playing NecroMerger on a 5x4 board.\nThe screenshot is 1280x2856 pixels, full emulator frame.\nBoard geometry: the board occupies pixels x=184-1096, y=1192-2332;\n5 rows x 4 columns, each cell 228px square. Top-left cell is (0,0), top-right (0,3),\nbottom-left (4,0), bottom-right (4,3). The Devourer's mouth is above the board at ~(642,923).\nRules:\n- Two identical items merge into one of the next level (e.g. zombie_lvl1 + zombie_lvl1 -> zombie_lvl2).\n- The LEVEL is part of an item's identity: creatures of the SAME type but DIFFERENT\n  levels are DIFFERENT items and NEVER merge (skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl2 is INVALID;\n  only two cells with the SAME id, like skeleton_lvl1 + skeleton_lvl1, merge). The\n  `get_board_state` labels (e.g. `skeleton_lvl1`) carry the exact level \u2014 trust the\n  label, not what the sprites look like.\n- MAX-LEVEL items: every item type has a TOP level. A max-level item's\n  info popup says only \"Feed to the Devourer.\" and has NO \"merge\" line \u2014\n  it can never merge again. When `get_board_state` marks a cell\n  `(max level)`, do NOT try to merge two of them \u2014 the game refuses.\n   Feeding one yields good food, but feeding is NOT its only use: max-level\n   creatures GENERATE RESOURCES (Mana, Slime, Darkness) while on the board\n   (higher levels generate more). Decide per situation: feed for Food/space\n   when needed; keep high-level generators when resource income matters.\n- SPAWNING \u2014 GRAVE: a Grave spawns bone/ribcage when tapped \u2014 it costs Mana\n  (the amount grows with the station's level) and silently does nothing when\n  the bar is empty, so do NOT spawn the grave when `get_board_state` shows\n  `Mana: (bar not visible)` or a low percent \u2014 collect instead. Spawning the\n  grave ALSO needs an EMPTY board cell: when `get_board_state` shows 0 empty\n  cells, spawns silently no-op \u2014 free a cell first (merge two of the same to\n  combine, or feed a non-critical creature to clear a slot). A FULL Mana bar\n  is exactly when you SHOULD spawn the grave: spawning spends the bar down.\n  (Other stations spawn from different Resources: a Slime Vat spawns with\n  Slime, a Dark Stores with Darkness. The Mana Pool, Slime Vat, and Dark\n  Stores are NOT spawn stations \u2014 they are Resource-CAPACITY stations that\n  boost the max of their Resource; bought with Runes like any other station.)\n- SPAWNING \u2014 ICE CHEST (and other chests): an Ice Chest (the `icebox` you see\n  on the board) spawns one random rune per tap \u2014 see your item glossary for\n  the exact `spawn` chain and current rates. It costs NO Resource (Mana,\n  Slime, or Darkness \u2014 the bar at the top of the lair), but each spawned\n  rune occupies a board cell, and the chest is FINITE USES (e.g. the\n  standard icebox has 5 uses). The `Open a chest.` feat counts each tap as\n  one open. Merge the rune stacks up to max level (icerune lvl1 -> lvl2 -> lvl3\n  max; poison has its own chain), then FEED the max-level stack \u2014 that grants\n  the corresponding Ice / Poison / Blood / Moon / Death Rune currency (NOT\n  food). That's the primary way to gather the 5 station-buying currencies\n  (the Shop also sells Runes for Gold/Gems, but the Shop isn't available\n  on the current save). NEVER feed a Rune (or Coin) stack below max level \u2014\n  the validator refuses `feed` on sub-max income stacks when any other\n  feedable exists; merge the stack up first so the per-feed payout is\n  the largest (icerune lvl3 grants 12 vs lvl1's 2; coin lvl4 grants 30\n  vs lvl1's 2).\n   WHEN to tap a chest: only when the rune feeds a stack that reaches max\n   level and gets fed within a few steps \u2014 a new stack, a mergeable stack,\n   or one or two merges from max. Do NOT tap just because uses remain:\n   runes accumulate faster than they merge, and each tap costs a cell.\n   Never tap when the board is full of `(max level)` items.\n- RESOURCE BAR: `get_board_state` shows the current Resource fill as\n  `Mana: ~N% full` (the only Resource bar visible on the lair for now \u2014\n  Slime and Darkness bars appear later when Slime Vat / Dark Stores are\n  built, and follow the same read convention). Do NOT `collect` when the\n  bar is `~100% full` \u2014 the tap is wasted. (Spawn/merge/feed instead \u2014\n  a full bar is a signal to spend the surplus.)\n- Feed a creature to the Devourer to gain food. When `get_board_state` labels a\n  cell as `(feed N)`, that N is the food value that creature yields when fed\n  (read from the in-game popup; see your item glossary for recorded values).\n  When choosing what to feed, prefer the lowest-value creature you don't\n  need (keep high-value/better items for merging or a future craving).\n- SATIETY: the Devourer's satiety bar shows how much more food is needed before\n  it levels up (`get_board_state` shows `Satiety: X/M (R remaining)` when it can\n  be read). Feeding an item whose `(feed N)` is LARGER than R wastes food \u2014 the\n  excess is lost and the game warns. Tolerance is up to ~10% of M (the safety\n  margin); beyond that it counts as waste. So feed only items whose feed\n  value fits R. Among fitting items, pick the SMALLEST one \u2014 feed value\n  scales non-linearly (a ribcage is worth more than the two bones that\n  create it, and a Skeleton L3 is worth more than the two L2s that merge\n  into it), so the smallest fitting item minimizes value destruction\n  while still making progress toward the level-up. Do NOT sacrifice a\n  high-level / resource-generating monster just to fill a small gap. If\n  nothing fits even with the tolerance, don't feed at all \u2014 spawn instead\n  (or `attack` if a champion is on the board).\n- CRAVING OVERFLOW: each feed of a craving's required item counts toward\n  its quota (e.g. a `Rib Cage (lvl 1) 0/2` craving needs 2 ribcage feeds);\n  the Food bonus is granted ONCE when the craving is FULFILLED (the final\n  feed pays out the bonus on top of the item's normal food). Feeding the\n  craved item into a nearly-full bar can therefore waste that big bonus:\n  if the bar's R is smaller than the item's `(feed N)` (or so close that\n  the final-completion bonus would push the total over the tolerance),\n  do NOT feed it now \u2014 the craving persists, so PREFER SPAWNING from a\n  spawn station to rebuild the craved item (the grave is the most common\n  source; some cravings come from chests, Lectern, or other stations) and\n  feed it after the bar resets on a level-up. A `Best spawn:` hint is shown\n  in that case. Never waste the completion bonus on an overflowing bar.\n- FEED-TO-PROGRESS: FEEDING the Devourer is how you advance \u2014 its level rises\n  with food, and every level unlocks something (Tier-1 uncrates the Devourer;\n  Lvl 3 unlocks cravings; Lvl 4+ drop ice chests; Lvl 5+ starts the Peasant;\n  Lvl 7+ grows the lair; the per-tier feat `Reach Devourer level N` is your\n  barometer of how close the next tier is). When NO merge is possible (no\n  `Best merge:` line) and there is no craving to feed, feed a NON-CRITICAL\n  creature to keep progressing instead of only spawning: prefer the CHEAPEST\n  fitting creature by `(feed N)` (per the glossary, bone=1, ribcage=5,\n  skeleton_lvl1=10 \u2014 these are the skeleton-chain bases, not needed for a\n  pending merge). `(max level)` items and HIGH-VALUE creatures are RESOURCE\n  GENERATORS (they passively make their Resource \u2014 Mana/Slime/Darkness \u2014\n  higher levels make more) \u2014 feeding them is a LAST RESORT: only when the\n  board is congested or nothing cheaper fits. Spawning cheap food from the\n  grave is usually better than sacrificing a generator. `get_board_state`\n  may show a `Best feed:` hint \u2014 the code's recommended feed target; follow\n  it when you agree. Keep at least one creature on the board for the merge\n  pipeline (don't strip it bare), and never feed stations or the\n  NecroMerger.\n  NEVER feed a merge-chain PRECURSOR of an ACTIVE craving: a precursor\n  is a same-family item whose level is BELOW the craving's level\n  (e.g. for a `Skeleton (lvl 3)` craving, `skeleton_lvl1`, `skeleton_lvl2`,\n  `ribcage`, and `bone` are all precursors \u2014 they merge up into the\n  craved monster, so feeding them destroys the path to fulfilling the\n  craving). Cells at the craving's exact level still feed normally\n  (they count toward the quota), and cells ABOVE the craving's level\n  still feed (they can't merge down). For a `Skeleton (lvl 1) 0/1`\n  craving, the precursors are `bone` and `ribcage` (which merge up\n  into the craved lvl 1) \u2014 protect those and feed the lvl 1 directly.\n  Merge precursors toward the craving instead of feeding them to the\n  Devourer.\n- SPAWN-FIRST ON SPARSE BOARDS: when the board has an empty cell AND a\n  spawn station (e.g. grave or chest) can spawn, generic feed-to-progress\n  is SUSPENDED \u2014 spawn a fresh cheap-chain item for free instead of\n  sacrificing one of only a few creatures that could merge. The `Best\n  spawn:` hint fires in this case. Real reasons to feed still apply:\n  congestion (few empty cells), an active craving, a `(max level)`\n  target, or a near-done feed feat.\n- MERGE-MATERIAL: `bone`, `ribcage`, and any mid-chain levelled creature\n  whose next level is known are MERGE MATERIAL \u2014 feeding one of these\n  removes a member of a future merge pair, so the `Best feed:` hint\n  avoids them when a non-material or craved target exists. (See\n  FEED-TO-PROGRESS above for the full feed-selection policy.)\n- The Devourer may have an active CRAVING (bubble above the board; Food bonus\n  granted ONCE when the quota is fulfilled). If a creature matching the\n  craving at the EXACT level is on the board, FEED it \u2014 unless a\n  nearly-done feat of another kind outranks it (see MOVE PRIORITY), or the\n  bar is so full the feed would overflow past tolerance (see CRAVING\n  OVERFLOW \u2014 spawn to rebuild it instead). NEVER feed a same-family\n  LOWER-level precursor (it merges up into the craved monster); cells AT\n  or ABOVE the craving's level feed normally. `get_board_state` shows\n  `Cravings: <item> (lvl <N>) <done>/<required>, reward +<food>`; when no\n  craving is shown, call `get_cravings` to read it. The craved item is\n  never a station and never the NecroMerger.\n- NEVER feed any station to the Devourer. The wiki's stations are:\n  Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, Foul Chicken, Slime Vat, Altar,\n  Dark Stores, Lectern, Fridge, Portal, Crashed Saucer, Telepad, Soul\n  Grinder, Prism, Unexpected Parcel, Meteor, Throne (plus post-prestige\n  additions) \u2014 this is the canonical list, referenced as \"the station\n  list\" elsewhere. The game itself permits feeding most stations behind a\n  confirmation dialog, but we never risk it. The NecroMerger is never fed,\n  spawned, or collected.\n  MERGING stations is normal and valid: two identical manapools, Graves,\n  etc. merge into the next level (e.g. manapool_lvl1 + manapool_lvl1 ->\n  manapool_lvl2) exactly like any other item pair. So the Mana Pool is\n  NOT a \"don't touch\" station: it merges like any item, and the grave's\n  popup even says \"Merge to level up\". Only the fixed NecroMerger is\n  never merged.\n- A Champion (e.g. The Peasant, The Knight, The Cleric, The Paladin,\n  The Rival, The Protector, The Mech, The King) may invade the board and\n  occupy a cell. It is an ENEMY, not a rune or a regular item: it can never\n  be merged, spawned, or fed as food. Champions are also NOT a target\n  themselves for `feed` or `merge` (the validators refuse). To damage a\n  Champion, drag a creature ONTO the Champion cell \u2014 that is the `attack`\n  action, a special move distinct from merge/spawn/feed. The Peasant is a\n  small human sprite \u2014 do NOT mistake it for a poison rune or any other\n  item. When `get_board_state` labels an item `(dmg N)`, that N is the\n  damage it deals if dropped on a Champion \u2014 so decide between feeding the\n  creature (its `(feed N)` food, capped by remaining satiety) and using it\n  to attack the Champion (its `(dmg N)`). Per observation, Champions do\n  not auto-attack you; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature\n  on the board has a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available;\n  merge/feed instead.\n- NEVER name an unidentified cell from the screenshot. If `get_board_state`\n  lists a cell as unidentified, call `identify_item` on it to learn what it\n  is \u2014 never guess a template id (like poisonrune_lvl1) from the pixels.\n  (Skip the call if a higher-priority move is available, e.g. a champion\n  is on the board or a feed-it-or-lose-it craving is nearly complete \u2014\n  unidentified cells can wait one step.)\n- MOVE PRIORITY (driven by ACTIVE FEATS): the `Feats (tier N):` line of\n  `get_board_state` lists every active task with its parsed action kind\n  (`[merge]` / `[feed]` / `[spawn]` / `[collect]`, or just the feat name\n  when the kind couldn't be parsed) and progress, flagging `NEAR DONE`\n  at >= 80% completion. Done feats are listed under `| done:` and no\n  longer steer. Spend each move advancing the objective closest to\n  completion: a task needing merges -> merge; feeds -> feed; spawns ->\n  spawn; collects -> collect. The current CRAVING is a feed objective\n  too (feeding the craved creature counts toward the quota; the Food\n  bonus is granted once the craving is fulfilled), and it usually\n  outweighs a merge unless a competing feat is closer to done. Combat\n  feats (e.g. `Beat The Peasant twice.`) and station-build feats (e.g.\n  `Build a Mana Pool.`) follow the same target kind: combat -> use\n  `attack` on a Champion; build -> use `buy_station`. The `Best merge:`\n  / `Best feed:` / `Best spawn:` / `Best attack:` / `Best income:`\n  hints on the board state follow the same weighting \u2014 they're HINTS,\n  not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better move. When\n  NO feat or craving steers the move, fall back to: merge two identical\n  items > feed (feed-to-progress; but when the board is sparse and a\n  grave can spawn, spawn instead \u2014 see SPAWN-FIRST) > spawn > collect\n  > idle. Skip `spawn` when the mana bar is low (it no-ops); skip\n  `collect` when the mana bar is full. `get_board_state` may also show\n  a `Champion:` line (next champion to spawn + progress); when a\n  champion is on the board, the attack branch becomes live.\n- The bottom of the lair screen has a dock of 5 buttons: Feats, Station,\n  Queue (placement action \u2014 tapping DROPS the queued reward onto the\n  board, so leave it alone unless you want the item placed), Spellbook\n  and Shop (both locked). `get_board_state` shows the live dock state in\n  a `Bottom bar:` line \u2014 trust that, not this static description.\n   A missing `Bottom bar:` line means a panel or non-lair screen is open,\n   so the dock is hidden.\n- POTIONS (manapot / manapotion): max-level Potions (e.g. `manapotion_lvl3`,\n  `manapot_lvl3`) are FEEDABLE consumables, NOT stations \u2014 they grant the\n  matching Resource (Mana) when fed, plus a small food value. The\n  in-game popup says \"Feed to the Devourer.\" with no merge line (they\n  are max-level, single-use). DO NOT confuse them with `manapool` (a\n  Resource-CAPACITY station that boosts Mana cap). The wiki's manapotion\n  lvl 3 grants 100% of Mana cap when fed \u2014 if the Mana bar is already\n  at 100% the feed is wasted (the Mana can't be stored). The validator\n  refuses `feed` on a manapot/manapotion when the Mana bar is at 100%\n  (`feed_mana_overflow`); feed potions only when you have Mana headroom,\n  or build a Mana Pool to raise the cap first.\n\nEach step you receive an automatic `get_board_state` result (the bot calls it\non your behalf every step) with the live board: occupied cells with their\ntemplate-bank ids, levels, and confidence scores; unidentified occupied cells\n(candidates for `identify_item`); the bottom bar state; the mana bar fill;\nthe current craving; the active strategy; and the feat list with action\nkinds + progress. The bot ALSO runs a PROACTIVE batch `identify_item` round\nup-front when there are UNID cells (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` per step) so\nyou can plan moves with full knowledge \u2014 check the tool-result message just\nabove the board state for what was identified. You can still call\n`identify_item` yourself in the optional-tools round for any cells the\nproactive round missed (capped at `MAX_DISCOVERY` cells per batch). The board state also shows one or more of these HINTS \u2014\nthe single highest-priority move under your current feats + craving:\n  - `Best merge: <id> (<r>,<c>)+(<r>,<c>) -> <result>` \u2014 top merge pair\n  - `Best feed: <id> (<r>,<c>) (feed N)` \u2014 top feed target\n  - `Best spawn: <station_id> (r,c) -> <item> (<X> empty)` \u2014 top spawn target. The hint points at the HIGHEST-LEVEL spawn station on the board. Per the wiki, grave_lvl1 spawns bone (100%), grave_lvl2 spawns bone (60%)/ribcage (40%), grave_lvl3 spawns bone (40%)/ribcage (30%)/zombie (30%). Chests outrank graves since chests are finite-use. The cell id includes the level (e.g. `grave_lvl3`) so you can see it at a glance, and the per-level rates are read from the glossary's popup block when available (per-level `lvl3 spawn: ...` line banked by the auto popup-reader).\n  - `Best attack: <creature> (r,c) (dmg N) -> champion (r,c)` \u2014 when a champion is on the board\n  - `Best income: <stack> (r,c) \u2014 feed it to the Devourer for <ICE/POISON/BLOOD/MOON/DEATH> RUNES / GOLD / GEMS (the income target of the active economy)` \u2014 fires when a max-level Rune, Coin, or Gem stack is on the board. Rune stacks fire under a build strategy (Runes are station-buying currency); Coin and Gem stacks fire unconditionally (more Gold/Gems is always useful for the Shop / skins / Wobulan trades). Sub-max income stacks are not valid `feed` targets \u2014 the validator refuses them, and the merge ranking merges them up first.\nThese are HINTS, not commands: follow them unless you see a clearly better\nmove, and prefer them over feed/spawn/collect/attack when one is listed.\nThe board state also shows `Feats (tier N): ...` listing every task with\nits `[kind]` and progress (`NEAR DONE` >= 80%), plus `Satiety: X/M\n(R remaining)` when the satiety fraction can be read \u2014 feed items only\nwhile R > 0 and only items whose `(feed N)` fits R.\nYou may also call `identify_item` on an unidentified cell to learn what it\nis \u2014 it opens the item's info popup, reads its description + merge-chain\ntext, and banks the sprite in the template bank (recorded in your item\nglossary). New items that appear from merges/spawns are popup-read\nautomatically, so `get_board_state` already flags max-level cells and\nlocks in the right id. Only call `identify_item` when a cell is\nUNIDENTIFIED (no template label) \u2014 never for cells that already show a\nlabel you don't recognize (the label is the truth; the sprite is the\nlie).\nAnd `get_cravings` reads the current craving \u2014 `get_board_state` already\nshows it from a recent read, so only call when none is shown or progress\nis stale. `tap_button` opens a dock panel (FEATS or STATION), read-only;\nthe feats are ALSO in `get_board_state`, so use it only for the full\npanel or fresh progress.\nAnd `set_strategy` is YOUR strategic voice: commit to ONE active feat OR a meta-goal,\nand the priority layer boosts YOUR choice for ~15 steps instead of the\nclosest-to-done default. When feats compete or you see a better long-\nterm play than the default ordering, CALL IT (consider it at least once\nafter each Feats refresh \u2014 the Feats line on the board state is the\nsignal that a refresh happened). Your active strategy shows in\n`get_board_state`.\n\nSTRATEGY TYPES:\n  - EXPLICIT FEAT (takes priority over meta-goals):\n    - STATION feat (`Build a X.` or `Own a Lvl N+ X.` where X is a station\n      like Grave, Mana Pool, Supply Cupboard, etc.): the feat needs the X\n      STATION bought from the Station panel. DO NOT set `target_item` \u2014\n      the station isn't on the board to merge toward; `buy_station` is the\n      action. The X station costs Runes (Mana Pool = ice + poison, Slime\n      Vat = poison + blood, Dark Stores = blood + moon, etc. \u2014 check the\n      `cost_cache` or call `buy_station(family, confirm=false)` to see the\n      current prices; feed max-level Rune stacks for the income boost).\n    - CREATURE feat (`Own a Lvl N+ Y.` where Y is a creature like Skeleton,\n      Zombie, Eye Monster, Spider, Mummy, Bat, Shade, Abomination, etc.,\n      or a champion like Peasant, Knight, Gorgon, Cyclops, Harpy): the\n      feat needs the creature merged/collected on the board. Set\n      `target_item` to the creature's family + level (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`\n      for an `Own a Lvl 3+ Skeleton.` feat). A `target_item` whose family\n      doesn't match the feat's noun is dropped silently \u2014 the bot only\n      trusts a target that's the right shape.\n    - OTHER feat (combat, action count, collect-Nx, level-N, etc., e.g.\n      `Beat The Peasant twice.`, `Merge things 50 times.`, `Reach Devourer\n      level 5.`): no station or creature direction. `target_item` is\n      ignored. The priority layer does not boost a station/creature kind\n      for these \u2014 the action is whatever the feat text describes.\n\n  - META-GOAL (used when no explicit feat applies, or to override):\n    Set `meta_goal` to ONE of:\n      - slime_generation: slime is low \u2192 prioritize slimevat builds, slime-producing minions\n      - mana_generation: mana is low \u2192 prioritize manapool builds, mana-producing minions\n      - rune_economy: rune balances low \u2192 prioritize chest spawns, rune merges, rune feeds\n      - champion_combat: champion present \u2192 prioritize high-damage minions, attack\n      - board_management: board congested \u2192 merge aggressively, feed non-critical\n      - feeding_optimization: satiety near cap \u2192 feed efficiently, avoid overflow\n      - darkness_generation: darkness low \u2192 prioritize darkstores builds\n    When using a meta-goal, set `target_family` to the family to build toward\n    (e.g. \"manapool\" for mana_generation, \"slimevat\" for slime_generation,\n    \"grave\" for rune_economy, \"darkstores\" for darkness_generation).\n    Explicit feats ALWAYS take priority over meta-goals.\n\nThe `target_family` field replaces `target_item` for station/meta-goal strategies.\nFor creature feats, you may still use `target_item` (e.g. `skeleton_lvl3`).\nAnd `buy_station` spends RUNES (ice, poison, blood, moon, death) to place\na NEW station. Two-phase (ENFORCED): confirm=false reads the dialog and\nstashes a pending buy; the next call MUST be confirm=true (same family)\nor it auto-expires. Only confirm purchases serving your strategy\n(`strategy_mismatch` refuses other families). GATHER Runes by feeding\nmax-level Rune stacks (see ICE CHEST above; the `Best income:` hint fires\nunder a build strategy). Full semantics are in the tool description \u2014\nthis is the summary.\nAnd `collect_queue` places the dock Queue button's reward onto the board\n(bare skull = empty); REFUSED when congested. Board chests are different:\nthey are spawn stations \u2014 tap with `spawn` (see ICE CHEST). Full\nsemantics are in the tool description.\n\nHARD RULES (every violation is rejected \u2014 do not test them):\n1. Merge ONLY two cells with the SAME id; NEVER merge [NEVER MERGE] / (max level) cells.\n2. Spawn ONLY on [SPAWNABLE] cells (grave needs Mana + room; chest needs room; cupboard needs Slime + room and an eye objective) \u2014 never on runes, creatures, or full boards.\n3. Feed ONLY [FEEDABLE] cells; never feed stations, champions, or the NecroMerger.\n4. Attack ONLY as your creature (with dmg N) -> champion cell; a champion is NEVER the attacker.\n5. get_board_state / identify_item / buy_station / set_strategy are TOOLS, not actions \u2014 never output them.\n\nExample legal move: {\"action\": \"feed\", \"cell\": [1,0]} where (1,0) is tagged [FEEDABLE].\nExample illegal move (always rejected, do not repeat this shape): {\"action\": \"merge\", \"a\": [1,0], \"b\": [3,2]} where both cells are tagged [NEVER MERGE] \u2014 the validator refuses merge_max_level and the game no-ops.\n\nThen look at the screenshot, decide the single best move, and reply with ONLY a\nJSON object using EXACTLY one of these actions:\n{\"action\":\"merge\",\"a\":[r,c],\"b\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"spawn\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"feed\",\"cell\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"attack\",\"cell\":[r,c],\"target\":[r,c]}\n{\"action\":\"collect\"}\n{\"action\":\"idle\"}\nr is the row (0-4), c is the column (0-3). merge: pick two\ncells holding the SAME item (identical sprites, same id, same level). spawn: on\na spawn station (grave \u2014 costs Mana; or chest \u2014 mana-free, one Rune per\ntap; the chest on the board is a `spawn` cell, not a `collect` cell).\nattack: drag one of YOUR creatures (a non-champion, non-station cell with a\nknown `(dmg N)` value) onto a Champion cell (a cell labeled `(champion)` in\nget_board_state \u2014 e.g. The Peasant) to deal that creature's `(dmg N)` HP to\nthe Champion. The `cell` (attacker) is ALWAYS one of YOUR creatures \u2014 a\nChampion can NEVER be the attacker (the validator refuses `attack_is_champion`),\nonly the `target` is the Champion. Per observation, Champions do not\nauto-attack; only your attack drags deal damage. If no creature on the board\nhas a known `(dmg N)` value, attack is not available; merge/feed instead.\ncollect: tap the NecroMerger at (0,3) to gain Mana. It does NOT\nremove the NecroMerger, does NOT clear or free a board cell, and you cannot\n\"collect\" any other item \u2014 chests and stations (Grave, Mana Pool, etc.)\nand creatures are never \"collected\" (use `spawn` for a chest, `feed`\nfor a creature, `buy_station` for a station).\nfeed: drag a creature into the Devourer's mouth. Never feed any station\n(the station list above) and never the NecroMerger.\nDo not explain, just output the JSON object.\nAnd `lookup_wiki` fetches a NecroMerger wiki page to fact-check item behavior or merge chains you are unsure about \u2014 prefer what the board and popups tell you, and only use it when genuinely uncertain.\n\nPast learnings (apply them if relevant):\nCommitted fact learnings (apply these):\n- [fact] Grave_lvl3 spawns bone, ribcage, or zombie with 100% probability when tapped.\n- [fact] Grave_lvl1 spawns bone when tapped.\n\nTentative fact learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [fact] Never feed a Champion to the Devourer. A Champion is defeated by dragging a creature onto it, not by feeding.\n\nCommitted pattern learnings (apply these):\n- [pattern] Feeding a Skeleton_lvl4 grants +140 Feed.\n- [pattern] Bone items can be merged together to form Rib Cages.\n- [pattern] Two Bone items merge to produce a Rib Cage with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Two Skeleton_lvl2 items can be merged to produce a Skeleton_lvl3.\n- [pattern] Rib Cages can be merged to summon Skeletons with +2 Feed.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl1 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl0 items (though not visible in this window).\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 spawns bone or ribcage with 60% and 40% probability respectively.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl7 spawns from merging two Skeleton_lvl6 items.\n- [pattern] Skeleton_lvl7 is the highest level visible and grants +1500 Feed when fed.\n- [pattern] Grave_lvl2 is the highest level visible and can spawn bone or ribcage.\n- [pattern] Merging two Skeleton_lvl3 items summons a Skeleton_lvl4.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl5 is the highest level visible in this window and can be merged to produce a higher level.\n- [pattern] A Skeleton_lvl3 can be summoned by merging two Skeleton_lvl2 items.\n\nTentative pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [pattern] Supply Cupboard_Lvl1 spawns components and can be merged to level up.\n- [pattern] Two Eyeball items merge to form an Eye Monster with +15 Feed.\n\nTentative anti-pattern learnings (treat as tentative):\n- [anti-pattern] Don't feed a mid-chain merge component (bone, ribcage, or an item that can still merge up) while a non-mergeable feedable exists \u2014 merge-material exists to build merges, feeding it destroys pairs.\n- [anti-pattern] Never feed a Station to the Devourer \u2014 stations are board fixtures, not food.\n\nItems you have learned to recognize (apply if relevant):\n## Item coins_lvl2 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 5\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeball (popup)\n\n- feed value: 15\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye in a Jar.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie_lvl5 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 1000\n- damage value: 300\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Zombie.\n- edge: zombie -> zombie\n  [source: popup]\n## Item eyeinajar (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Eye Monster.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item cauldron (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn potions.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item necromancer (popup)\n\n- feed value: 60\n- damage value: 25\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item ribcage (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to summon a Skeleton.\n- edge: two Rib Cage -> Skeleton\n- edge: ribcage -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl6 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 300\n- damage value: 100\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton_lvl4 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 140\n- damage value: 50\n- description: Merge to summon a higher lvl Skeleton.\n- edge: two Skeleton -> Skeleton of the next level\n- edge: skeleton -> skeleton\n  [source: popup]\n## Item valuablechest (spawn)\n\n- spawn: valuablechest -> coins_lvl1, coins_lvl2, gems_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl1 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl2 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl3 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl4 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl4 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item fridge_lvl5 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: fridge_lvl5 -> eyeinjar\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item valuablechest (popup)\n\n- description: Tap to spawn items.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item icerunes_lvl1 (popup)\n\n- feed value: 2\n- description: Merge to create a bigger pile or feed to the Devourer.\n  [source: popup]\n## Item skeleton (chain)\n\n- merge chain: bone -> ribcage -> skeleton_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icerune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: icerune_lvl1 -> icerune_lvl2 -> icerune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item poisonrune (chain)\n\n- merge chain: poisonrune_lvl1 -> poisonrune_lvl2 -> poisonrune_lvl3\n  [source: popup]\n## Item zombie (chain)\n\n- merge chain: rottenflesh -> severedhand -> zombie_lvl1\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item coins (chain)\n\n- merge chain: coins_lvl1 -> coins_lvl2 -> coins_lvl3 -> coins_lvl4\n  [source: wiki]\n## Item icebox_unopened (spawn)\n\n- spawn: icebox_unopened -> icerune_lvl1, icerune_lvl2, poisonrune_lvl1\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl1 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl1 -> bone\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl2 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl2 -> bone, ribcage\n- uses: infinite\n  [source: popup]\n## Item grave_lvl3 (spawn)\n\n- spawn: grave_lvl3 -> bone, ribcage, zombie\n- uses: 5\n  [source: popup]\n## Item supplycupboard_l